lity guidance. EDTA chelates metal ions. This may matter if the protein requires metal cofactors or if metal-catalyzed reactions are relevant. Sodium chloride/saline contributes ionic strength and can affect solubility and protein interactions; its effect depends on concentration and protein properties. A detergent/surfactant may reduce surface adsorption or aggregation in some formulations; the exact purpose and compatibility depend on the specific ingredient and concentration. Bicarbonate can contribute to pH buffering, but its behaviour may depend on the solution's gas exposure and formulation. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.","– Section 10: Buffers and stock solutions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 87 | Prepare transfer buffer. | Transfer the membrane to 1 x transfer buffer. | Add sample buffer (SDS and β mercaptoethanol). Heat 95˚C 5 min. | the antibody datasheet, and buffers without detergent or with relatively mild non-ionic | For buffer recipes, please see the Buffers section 10 beginning on page 87. | Nonidet-P40 (NP40) buffer | This is a popular buffer for studying proteins that are cytoplasmic, or membrane-bound, or | extracted from insoluble material or aggregates, RIPA buffer may be more suitable, as it | This buffer is also useful for whole cell extracts and membrane-bound proteins, and may | be preferable to NP-40 or Triton X-100-only buffers for extracting nuclear proteins than | buffers containing only NP-40 or Triton X-100. It will disrupt protein-protein interactions and | recognize a non-denatured epitope), a buffer without ionic detergents (e.g. SDS) | with detergent-free buffer is achieved by mechanical shearing, often with a Dounce | buffer will suffice, but as noted above, buffers with detergents are required to release | The buffer (with inhibitors) should be ice-cold prior to h