Version 6f Last updated 05 August 2024,glucose oxidase,K9L4P7,Abcam,"The antibody to be labeled should be purified, in an appropriate buffer for conjugation and at | a suitable concentration conjugation (as described in buffer considerations). | ready antibodies are affinity purified, manufactured in a buffer free from BSA, sodium azide and | If the antibody of choice is supplied in a buffer not compatible with conjugation (as described | in buffer considerations), we offer a dedicated range of antibody purification and | Buffer considerations: | Recommended pre-conjugation buffer components and conditions: | Buffer Components & Conditions | Amine free buffer (e.g. MES, MOPS, HEPES, PBS) | Non-buffering salts (e.g. sodium chloride) | Borate buffer | Yes",,"PBS/phosphate-buffered saline generally provides pH buffering and salt conditions intended to keep the protein in an aqueous, near-physiological environment; the exact suitability depends on the product and concentration. HEPES is a pH buffer often used to help maintain solution pH; its presence alone does not establish protein stability or a storage temperature. Albumin may act as a carrier or stabilizer and can reduce non-specific loss of dilute proteins; confirm the role from the manufacturer's formulation notes. Sodium azide is commonly used as a preservative to limit microbial growth. It is hazardous and can affect downstream applications; follow the product safety sheet and assay compatibility guidance. Sodium chloride/saline contributes ionic strength and can affect solubility and protein interactions; its effect depends on concentration and protein properties. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.","The antibody to be labeled should be purified, in an appropriate buffer for conjugation and at | a suitable concentration conjugation (as described in buffer considerations). | ready antibodies are a