4°C",,storage,"[handling] Reconstitution: We recommend that the vial be briefly centrifuged prior to opening to bring the contents to the bottom. Lyophilized hG-CSF should be reconstituted in sterile deionized water to 0.1-1.0 mg/mL to regain full activity. These stock solutions should be apportioned into working aliquots and stored at ≤-20°C. Further dilutions should be made in low endotoxin medium or buffered solution with FBS or tissue culture grade BSA. Storage: Lyophilized Hg-CSF should be stored at 2°C to 8°C, preferably desiccated. Store reconstituted hG-CSF at ≤-20°C (not in a frost-free freezer). Keep freeze-thaw cycles to a minimum.",webpage,https://www.thermofisher.com/proteins/product/Human-G-CSF-Recombinant-Protein/PHC2031
Iranian Biomedical Journal 19 (2): 102-110 (April 2015),granulocyte colony-stimulating factor G-CSF,,WHO,"phosphate buffer (pH 7.4). The wet cells (50 g per 200 | body per 5 ml) was resuspended in first washing buffer | (2.5 g l-1 Triton X-100, 50 mM Tris-HCl, 5 mM | was resuspended in second washing buffer (2 M urea) | solubilization buffer (30 mM Tris-HCl [pH 12], 1 mM | refolding buffer (3 mg protein per ml refolding buffer | with stirring), containing 30 mM Tris-HCl (pH 5), 3 M | bed volume of buffer A (20 mM Na-acetate, pH 5.0) at | 4 ml/min. A 50-ml linear gradient to 100% buffer B (1 | M NaCl in buffer A) was applied at 2 ml/min. The | which had equilibrated with GFC buffer (20 mM | performed with buffer A at 0.8 ml/min, and 2.4-ml | analyzed with 10-µM protein in 10 mM citrate buffer | (20.5 ml citric acid and 29.5 ml sodium citrate, pH 5) | citrate buffer (pH 5) was consistent with the previously | buffer at pH 5. | the analysis of rhG-CSF in pharmaceutical formulations.",7.4; 12; 5; 5.0,"Tris/Tris-HCl is a pH-buffering system. The reported pH and concentration are needed to judge the actual formulation. Phosphate salts commonly help control pH and contribute ionic strength; exact effects depend on pH, concentration, and other ingredients. Sodium chloride/saline contributes ionic strength and can affect solubility and protein interactions; its effect depends on concentration and protein properties. A detergent/surfactant may reduce surface adsorption or aggregation in some formulations; the exact purpose and compatibility depend on the specific ingredient and concentration. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.","phosphate buffer (pH 7.4). The wet cells (50 g per 200 | body per 5 ml) was resuspended in first washing buffer | (2.5 g l-1 Triton X-100, 50 mM Tris-HCl, 5 mM | was resuspended in second washing buffer (2 M urea) | solubilization buffer