ab270883 – Human IL-2 SimpleStep ELISA® Kit,interleukin 2 IL-2,,Abcam,Detector Antibodies have only been tested for stability in the provided 10X formulations. | Sample Diluent Buffer | N=,,"The source reports a buffer/formulation, but its ingredients are not recognized by this simple interpretation rule set. Use the exact composition, pH, concentrations, and manufacturer's technical notes to interpret it. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.",Detector Antibodies have only been tested for stability in the provided 10X formulations. | Sample Diluent Buffer | N=,2-8°C; 4°C,Cold chain indicated,Storage-context temperature at or below 8 °C was extracted (2-8°C; 4°C); verify the exact manufacturer instruction.,,"Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. Refer to list of materials | Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. | supplied for storage conditions of individual components. Observe the storage conditions for | Storage | pack, resealed and stored at 4°C. | containing the desiccant pack, reseal and return to 4ºC storage. | Storage Condition",,,"Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. Refer to list of materials | Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. | supplied for storage conditions of individual components. Observe the storage conditions for | Storage | pack, resealed and stored at 4°C. | containing the desiccant pack, reseal and return to 4ºC storage. | Storage Condition","Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. Refer to list of materials | Storage and Stability: Store kit at 2-8ºC immediately upon receipt. | supplied for storage conditions of individual components. Observe the storage conditions for | Storage | pack, resealed and stored at 4°C. | containing the desiccant pack, reseal and return to 4ºC storage. | Storage Condition","1, 2, 3, 5",storage,"Page 1 [unclassified]: +4°C (temperature: +4°C) | Page 1 [unclassified]: Equilibrate all reagents to room temperature (18-25°C) prior to use. (temperature: 18-25°C) | Page 1 [unclassified]: gentle mixing, the precipitate may be dissolved by gentle warming and mixing at 37°C for 10 (temperature: 37°C) | Page 2 [experimental]: centrifuge samples at 2,000 x g for 10 minutes and collect serum. Dilute samples at least 1:4 into Store un-diluted serum at -20ºC or below. (temperature: -20°C) | Page 2 [experimental]: minutes. Dilute samples at least 1:4 into Sample Diluent 50BP and assay. Store un-diluted plasma samples at -20ºC or below for up to 3 months. (temperature: -20°C) | Page 2 [experimental]: debris. Collect supernatants Assay, or dilute samples into Sample Diluent NS and assay. Store un-diluted samples at -20°C or below. (temperature: -20°C) | Page 5 [unclassified]: +4°C (temperature: +4°C) | Page 5 [unclassified]: Equilibrate all reagents to room temperature (18-25°C) prior to use. (temperature: 18-25°C) | Page 5 [unclassified]: gentle mixing, the precipitate may be dissolved by gentle warming and mixing at 37°C for 10 (temperature: 37°C)",PDF,https://content.abcam.com/content/dam/abcam/product/documents/270/ab270883/Human-IL-2-ELISA-Kit-protocol-book-v4a-ab270883%20(website).pdf
Change to cold-storage conditions could make Pfizer COVID-19 vaccine more widely available,interleukin 2 IL-2,,Gavi,,,,,2-8°C; -80°C; -60°C; -20°C,Cold chain indicated,Explicit refrigerated or frozen-storage wording appears in the storage evidence.,"Several regulators have agreed the mRNA vaccine can be kept refrigerated for up to 30 days, making it easier to distribute in lower-resource settings. | <div><p>Several regulators have agreed the mRNA vaccine can be kept refrigerated for up to 30 days, making it easier to distribute in lower-resource settings.</p> | </blockquote> <p>The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage.</p> <div> | </div> <p>BioNTech told <a href=""https://www.reuters.com/article/us-health-coronavirus-pfizer-idUSKBN2AJ1CJ"">Reuters</a> that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted <a href=""https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-brief-fda-authorizes-longer-time-refrigerator-storage-thawed-pfizer-biontech-covid-19-vaccine"">further data</a> to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month.</p> <p>Increased flexibility in the storage and handling of the vaccine is expected to have a significant impact on its <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/covax-vaccine-roll-out"" title=""COVAX vaccine roll-out"">roll-out</a> in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too.</p> <p>Moderna’s <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/what-are-nucleic-acid-vaccines-and-how-could-they-be-used-against-covid-19"" title=""What are nucleic acid vaccines and how could they be used against COVID-19?"">mRNA vaccine</a>, for which COVAX recently signed an <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/news/media-room/gavi-signs-agreement-moderna-secure-doses-behalf-covax-facility"" title=""Gavi signs agreement with Moderna to secure doses on behalf of COVAX Facility"">advance purchase agreement</a> for 500 million doses, has less stringent cold-chain requirements: it must be shipped at -20°C (-4°F) and can be stored at that temperature for up to six months, but once thawed, it too can be kept in a standard refrigerator at 2-8°C (36-46°F) for up to 30 days.</p> </div> </div> | The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage. | that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted | to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month. | in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too. | for 500 million doses, has less stringent cold-chain requirements: it must be shipped at -20°C (-4°F) and can be stored at that temperature for up to six months, but once thawed, it too can be kept in a standard refrigerator at 2-8°C (36-46°F) for up to 30 days.","Change to cold-storage conditions could make Pfizer COVID-19 vaccine more widely available | <span>Change to cold-storage conditions could make Pfizer COVID-19 vaccine more widely available</span> | <div> <div> <div> <div> <p>Both the European Medicines Agency (EMA) and US Food and Drugs Administration (FDA) have authorised a longer time during which the Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccine could be kept in a standard refrigerator – a development which should make the vaccine more widely available, now that the World Health Organization (WHO) has followed suit.</p> <p>The <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/covax-explained"" title=""COVAX explained"">COVAX initiative</a>, co-led by Gavi, has secured up to 40 million doses of Pfizer-BioNTech’s vaccine, to be delivered during 2021. However, the complexities of the vaccine, including the need for ultra-low temperature storage, have limited its distribution to just <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/news/document-library/covax-first-round-allocation-AZ-SII"" title=""COVAX first round allocation of AZ and SII"">18 countries</a>, until now.</p> <blockquote> | <h2>The vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too.</h2> | </blockquote> <p>The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage.</p> <div> | <h4>Have you read?</h4> <ul> <li><a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/long-view-keeping-vaccines-cool-cold-chain"" title=""The Long View: Keeping vaccines cool with cold chain"">The Long View: Keeping vaccines cool with cold chain</a></li> <li><a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/can-we-stop-wearing-masks-after-being-vaccinated"" title=""Can we stop wearing masks after being vaccinated?"">Can we stop wearing masks after being vaccinated?</a></li> <li><a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/self-covid-19-tests-do-you-know-your-tonsils-your-uvula"" title=""Self-COVID-19 tests: do you know your tonsils from your uvula?"">Self-COVID-19 tests: do you know your tonsils from your uvula?</a></li> | </div> <p>BioNTech told <a href=""https://www.reuters.com/article/us-health-coronavirus-pfizer-idUSKBN2AJ1CJ"">Reuters</a> that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted <a href=""https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-brief-fda-authorizes-longer-time-refrigerator-storage-thawed-pfizer-biontech-covid-19-vaccine"">further data</a> to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month.</p> <p>Increased flexibility in the storage and handling of the vaccine is expected to have a significant impact on its <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/covax-vaccine-roll-out"" title=""COVAX vaccine roll-out"">roll-out</a> in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too.</p> <p>Moderna’s <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/what-are-nucleic-acid-vaccines-and-how-could-they-be-used-against-covid-19"" title=""What are nucleic acid vaccines and how could they be used against COVID-19?"">mRNA vaccine</a>, for which COVAX recently signed an <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/news/media-room/gavi-signs-agreement-moderna-secure-doses-behalf-covax-facility"" title=""Gavi signs agreement with Moderna to secure doses on behalf of COVAX Facility"">advance purchase agreement</a> for 500 million doses, has less stringent cold-chain requirements: it must be shipped at -20°C (-4°F) and can be stored at that temperature for up to six months, but once thawed, it too can be kept in a standard refrigerator at 2-8°C (36-46°F) for up to 30 days.</p> </div> </div> | <a target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener noreferrer"" href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/change-cold-storage-conditions-could-make-pfizer-covid-19-vaccine-more-widely"">Original article</a> | , co-led by Gavi, has secured up to 40 million doses of Pfizer-BioNTech’s vaccine, to be delivered during 2021. However, the complexities of the vaccine, including the need for ultra-low temperature storage, have limited its distribution to just | The vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too. | The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage. | The Long View: Keeping vaccines cool with cold chain | Increased flexibility in the storage and handling of the vaccine is expected to have a significant impact on its | in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too. | for 500 million doses, has less stringent cold-chain requirements: it must be shipped at -20°C (-4°F) and can be stored at that temperature for up to six months, but once thawed, it too can be kept in a standard refrigerator at 2-8°C (36-46°F) for up to 30 days.","</div> <p>BioNTech told <a href=""https://www.reuters.com/article/us-health-coronavirus-pfizer-idUSKBN2AJ1CJ"">Reuters</a> that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted <a href=""https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-brief-fda-authorizes-longer-time-refrigerator-storage-thawed-pfizer-biontech-covid-19-vaccine"">further data</a> to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month.</p> <p>Increased flexibility in the storage and handling of the vaccine is expected to have a significant impact on its <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/covax-vaccine-roll-out"" title=""COVAX vaccine roll-out"">roll-out</a> in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too.</p> <p>Moderna’s <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/what-are-nucleic-acid-vaccines-and-how-could-they-be-used-against-covid-19"" title=""What are nucleic acid vaccines and how could they be used against COVID-19?"">mRNA vaccine</a>, for which COVAX recently signed an <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/news/media-room/gavi-signs-agreement-moderna-secure-d