d be stored at –20°C. All the other components of the kit should be kept | enzyme and opr0036 should be stored at –20°C. | enzyme and opr0036 should be stored at –20°C. All the other components of the kit should be kept at +4°C. | on ice until use, or storage at -20o C. | Note that: the concentration of CaCl2 in reconstituted Reaction Buffer is 11,6 mM. | For 1 x 8 strips microtiter wells: Add 10 µL of DTT per 0,5 mL of reconstituted Reaction","Storage and Stability | enzyme and opr0036 should be stored at –20°C. All the other components of the kit should be kept | enzyme and opr0036 should be stored at –20°C. | enzyme and opr0036 should be stored at –20°C. All the other components of the kit should be kept at +4°C. | on ice until use, or storage at -20o C. | Note that: the concentration of CaCl2 in reconstituted Reaction Buffer is 11,6 mM. | For 1 x 8 strips microtiter wells: Add 10 µL of DTT per 0,5 mL of reconstituted Reaction",4,storage,"Page 4 [shipping]: The kit is shipped on ice and storage at +4°C is recommended. Upon arrival, the DTT, SAv-HRP | Page 4 [shipping]: The kit is shipped on ice and storage at +4°C is recommended. (temperature: +4°C) | Page 5 [experimental]: Incubate 15 minutes at 37°C. (temperature: 37°C) | Page 5 [experimental]: Incubate for 15-30 minutes at 37°C and shake gently. (temperature: 37°C) | Page 5 [experimental]: Incubate for 15 minutes at 37°C and shake gently. (temperature: 37°C) | Page 6 [unclassified]: 6.0 at 37°C] have shown that 50 mU/mL correspond to an absorbance value (OD) of 1.4 + 0.06 (temperature: 37°C)",PDF,https://resources.bio-techne.com/products/documents/manual/Manual-NBP1-37008-97952690.pdf
Change to cold-storage conditions could make Pfizer COVID-19 vaccine more widely available,lysozyme,O75951,Gavi,,,,,2-8°C; -80°C; -60°C; -20°C,Cold chain indicated,Explicit refrigerated or frozen-storage wording appears in the storage evidence.,"Several regulators have agreed the mRNA vaccine can be kept refrigerated for up to 30 days, making it easier to distribute in lower-resource settings. | <div><p>Several regulators have agreed the mRNA vaccine can be kept refrigerated for up to 30 days, making it easier to distribute in lower-resource settings.</p> | </blockquote> <p>The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage.</p> <div> | </div> <p>BioNTech told <a href=""https://www.reuters.com/article/us-health-coronavirus-pfizer-idUSKBN2AJ1CJ"">Reuters</a> that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted <a href=""https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-brief-fda-authorizes-longer-time-refrigerator-storage-thawed-pfizer-biontech-covid-19-vaccine"">further data</a> to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month.</p> <p>Increased flexibility in the storage and handling of the vaccine is expected to have a significant impact on its <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/covax-vaccine-roll-out"" title=""COVAX vaccine roll-out"">roll-out</a> in EU member states, and within the USA. And now that the WHO has made a similar recommendation and updated its guidance, the vaccine can be stored at higher temperatures in Gavi-supported countries too.</p> <p>Moderna’s <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/what-are-nucleic-acid-vaccines-and-how-could-they-be-used-against-covid-19"" title=""What are nucleic acid vaccines and how could they be used against COVID-19?"">mRNA vaccine</a>, for which COVAX recently signed an <a href=""https://www.gavi.org/news/media-room/gavi-signs-agreement-moderna-secure-doses-behalf-covax-facility"" title=""Gavi signs agreement with Moderna to secure doses on behalf of COVAX Facility"">advance purchase agreement</a> for 500 million doses, has less stringent cold-chain requirements: it must be shipped at -20°C (-4°F) and can be stored at that temperature for up to six months, but once thawed, it too can be kept in a standard refrigerator at 2-8°C (36-46°F) for up to 30 days.</p> </div> </div> | The vaccine was the first to receive WHO Emergency Use Listing on 31 December 2020, but according to data submitted as part of this application, the vaccine had to be shipped at temperatures between -80°C and -60°C (-112°F and -76°F), could only be kept in a normal freezer at -20°C (-4°F) for two weeks, and unopened vials could only be stored at refrigerated temperatures of between 2-8°C for five days, before either being used or thrown away. Very few countries had the capacity to manage a vaccine requiring ultra-cold storage. | that it had initially imposed these requirements as a precautionary measure, because stability and durability tests of the vaccine had been started relatively late in the development process. However, its manufacturing partner Pfizer has since submitted | to regulators suggesting that undiluted, thawed vials of the vaccine are stable at refrigerator temperatures for up to one month. | in EU member states, and within the USA. And no