How are vaccines developed and produced?,trypsin,P07477,WHO,"There are usually multiple trials in this phase to evaluate various age groups and different formulations of the vaccine. A group that did not get the vaccine is usually included as a comparator group to determine whether the changes in the vaccinated group are attributed to the vaccine, or have happened by chance.",,"The source reports a buffer/formulation, but its ingredients are not recognized by this simple interpretation rule set. Use the exact composition, pH, concentrations, and manufacturer's technical notes to interpret it. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.","There are usually multiple trials in this phase to evaluate various age groups and different formulations of the vaccine. A group that did not get the vaccine is usually included as a comparator group to determine whether the changes in the vaccinated group are attributed to the vaccine, or have happened by chance.",8 °C; -20°C; -80°C; 8°C,Cold chain indicated,Explicit refrigerated or frozen-storage wording appears in the storage evidence.,,"When a vaccine is too hot or too cold, it becomes less effective or even inactive. If stored at the incorrect temperature, vaccines can be ruined or unsafe for use. Most vaccines require refrigerated storage at between 2 and 8 °C. Some vaccines | require temperatures as cold as -20°C. Some of the newer vaccines need to be kept ultra cold at  -80°C.  For frozen vaccines some of them can be safely stored for a limited time between 2 and 8°C.",,,"When a vaccine is too hot or too cold, it becomes less effective or even inactive. If stored at the incorrect temperature, vaccines can be ruined or unsafe for use. Most vaccines require refrigerated storage at between 2 and 8 °C. Some vaccines
require temperatures as cold as -20°C. Some of the newer vaccines need to be kept ultra cold at  -80°C.  For frozen vaccines some of them can be safely stored for a limited time between 2 and 8°C.
Regular refrigerators cannot maintain an even temperature consistently, so specialized medical refrigerators are required for these precious products.
How vaccines are shipped","When a vaccine is too hot or too cold, it becomes less effective or even inactive. If stored at the incorrect temperature, vaccines can be ruined or unsafe for use. Most vaccines require refrigerated storage at between 2 and 8 °C. Some vaccines
require temperatures as cold as -20°C. Some of the newer vaccines need to be kept ultra cold at  -80°C.  For frozen vaccines some of them can be safely stored for a limited time between 2 and 8°C.
Regular refrigerators cannot maintain an even temperature consistently, so specialized medical refrigerators are required for these precious products.
