"Ethiopia, Norway and UK commit US$240 million to CEPIâs global plan to tackle epidemic and pandemic threats",Penton base,P12538,CEPI,,,,,,Not specified,No explicit refrigerated/frozen requirement or interpretable storage temperature was found in the extracted storage evidence.,". Left unchecked, outbreaks of viruses such as Lassa, Nipah, Rift Valley fever and as-yet-unknown pathogens â collectively known as Disease X â represent an escalating threat to human life, global health systems and economic stability. | At the same time, the world is better equipped than ever to respond â if it chooses to invest. Advances in vaccinology, genomics, artificial intelligence and biomanufacturing have unlocked the possibility of developing safe, effective and accessible vaccines against new pandemic threats in as little as 100 days of their identification. This goal, known as the 100 Days Mission and embraced by G7 and G20 leaders, sits at the heart of CEPI 3.0. | The need for sustained investment in preparedness is underscored by the current outbreak of Bundibugyo ebolavirus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. CEPI mobilised human, technical and financial resources within days of the outbreak being declared an international emergency and is closely supporting the nationally-led research response, alongside the World Health Organization, Africa CDC and multiple other agencies. CEPI is backing the urgent development of five Bundibugyo virus vaccine candidates, with the goal of advancing safe and effective vaccines for use in this epidemic and to strengthen readiness for future outbreaks.",,,,". Left unchecked, outbreaks of viruses such as Lassa, Nipah, Rift Valley fever and as-yet-unknown pathogens â collectively known as Disease X â represent an escalating threat to human life, global health systems and economic stability.
At the same time, the world is better equipped than ever to respond â if it chooses to invest. Advances in vaccinology, genomics, artificial intelligence and biomanufacturing have unlocked the possibility of developing safe, effective and accessible vaccines against new pandemic threats in as little as 100 days of their identification. This goal, known as the 100 Days Mission and embraced by G7 and G20 leaders, sits at the heart of CEPI 3.0.
To deliver this step-change in preparedness, the CEPI 3.0 strategy centres on three interconnected priorities: developing vaccines to tackle the most dangerous pathogens and viral families; advancing rapid-response vaccine platform technologies capable of being swiftly adapted to unknown viruses; and strengthening global networks for research, manufacturing and regulatory readiness that can be activated swiftly whenever and wherever future threats emerge.
CEPI 3.0 and the Bundibugyo outbreak response
The need for sustained investment in preparedness is underscored by the current outbreak of Bundibugyo ebolavirus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. CEPI mobilised human, technical and financial resources within days of the outbreak being declared an international emergency and is closely supporting the nationally-led research response, alongside the World Health Organization, Africa CDC and multiple other agencies. CEPI is backing the urgent development of five Bundibugyo virus vaccine candidates, with the goal of advancing safe and effective vaccines for use in this epidemic and to strengthen readiness for future outbreaks.
The speed of this response is made possible by CEPIâs prior investments in rapid-response vaccine platforms, R&D networks and partnerships with world-leading researchers. CEPI 3.0âs core priorities â a viral family approach to vaccine development, readiness across multiple technology platforms, and networks and capabilities that can be rapidly activated â are critical to the Bundibugyo response today and to CEPIâs mission to prepare for the viral threats of tomorrow.",". Left unchecked, outbreaks of viruses such as Lassa, Nipah, Rift Valley fever and as-yet-unknown pathogens â collectively known as Disease X â represent an escalating threat to human life, global health systems and economic stability.
At the same time, the world is better equipped than ever to respond â if it chooses to invest. Advances in vaccinology, genomics, artificial intelligence and biomanufacturing have unlocked the possibility of developing safe, effective and accessible vaccines against new pandemic threats in as little as 100 days of their identification. This goal, known as the 100 Days Mission and embraced by G7 and G20 leaders, sits at the heart of CEPI 3.0.
To deliver this step-change in preparedness, the CEPI 3.0 strategy centres on three interconnected priorities: developing vaccines to tackle the most dangerous pathogens and viral families; advancing rapid-response vaccine platform technologies capable of being swiftly adapted to unknown viruses; and strengthening global networks for research, manufacturing and regulatory readiness that can be activated swiftly whenever and wherever future threats emerge.
