GFP (green fluorescent protein): a complete guide,green fluorescent protein GFP,A0A5J6CYR6,Abcam,,,,,,Not specified,No explicit refrigerated/frozen requirement or interpretable storage temperature was found in the extracted storage evidence.,"GFP has an 11-stranded beta-barrel structure, sometimes called a ""beta-can"", which provides stability that helps GFP resist moderate changes in pH, temperature, and some denaturants such as urea. This rigid cage shields the chromophore from solvent quenching and fluctuations in molecular oxygen, which is essential for stable fluorescence. | GFP is widely used because it combines high stability with intrinsic fluorescence production without requiring added substrates or enzymes. Oxygen is the only external requirement for chromophore formation, making GFP compatible with many living systems. Researchers can therefore use GFP to monitor biological processes in real time with limited disruption to normal cellular activity. | Photostability | Bajar, B. T. et al. Improving brightness and photostability of green and red fluorescent proteins for live-cell imaging and FRET reporting. Sci. Rep. 6, 20889 (2016).",,,,"GFP has an 11-stranded beta-barrel structure, sometimes called a ""beta-can"", which provides stability that helps GFP resist moderate changes in pH, temperature, and some denaturants such as urea. This rigid cage shields the chromophore from solvent quenching and fluctuations in molecular oxygen, which is essential for stable fluorescence.
2.
Chromophore
The GFP chromophore sits at the center of the beta-barrel and forms through the spontaneous cyclization and oxidation of three amino acid residues (Ser65–Tyr66–Gly67).
Because chromophore formation is autocatalytic, unlike luciferase-based reporters, GFP does not require added enzymes, substrates, or cofactors to fluoresce. It does, however, require oxygen.
Why is GFP important in research?
GFP is widely used because it combines high stability with intrinsic fluorescence production without requiring added substrates or enzymes. Oxygen is the only external requirement for chromophore formation, making GFP compatible with many living systems. Researchers can therefore use GFP to monitor biological processes in real time with limited disruption to normal cellular activity.
The 2008 Nobel Prize in Chemistry recognized the discovery and development of GFP. The prize was awarded to Osamu Shimomura, Martin Chalfie, and Roger Tsien.
Key milestones in the development of GFP include:
GFP discovery and isolation (1962)
: Osamu Shimomura isolates GFP from
Aequorea victoria
Photostability
For long time-lapse experiments, consider mClover3, which is 60% more photostable than Clover.
Folding efficiency
sfGFP is preferred for fusion proteins and aggregation-prone proteins.
pH sensitivity
YFP derivatives lose fluorescence at acidic pH.
Bajar, B. T. et al. Improving brightness and photostability of green and red fluorescent proteins for live-cell imaging and FRET reporting. Sci. Rep. 6, 20889 (2016).
