CEPI 3.0 cross-cutting themes explained: Biosecurity,insulin,P01308,CEPI,,,,,,Not specified,No explicit refrigerated/frozen requirement or interpretable storage temperature was found in the extracted storage evidence.,", we have to move fast, and a critical ingredient for moving fast is operating from the same rulebook, no matter where the outbreak emerges or whether it arises naturally, accidentally or deliberately. Biosecurity considerations run throughout the 100 Days Mission pathway, from laboratories developing and testing preclinical candidates, to teams evaluating early-stage clinical candidates, to the collection, testing, transfer and storage of specimens during clinical trials. A big part of our work is defining those rules before an emergency so research can move quickly and safely. | What role does biosecurity play in turning the 100 Days Mission into reality?",", we have to move fast, and a critical ingredient for moving fast is operating from the same rulebook, no matter where the outbreak emerges or whether it arises naturally, accidentally or deliberately. Biosecurity considerations run throughout the 100 Days Mission pathway, from laboratories developing and testing preclinical candidates, to teams evaluating early-stage clinical candidates, to the collection, testing, transfer and storage of specimens during clinical trials. A big part of our work is defining those rules before an emergency so research can move quickly and safely.",,,", we have to move fast, and a critical ingredient for moving fast is operating from the same rulebook, no matter where the outbreak emerges or whether it arises naturally, accidentally or deliberately. Biosecurity considerations run throughout the 100 Days Mission pathway, from laboratories developing and testing preclinical candidates, to teams evaluating early-stage clinical candidates, to the collection, testing, transfer and storage of specimens during clinical trials. A big part of our work is defining those rules before an emergency so research can move quickly and safely.
As a funder of research involving high-consequence pathogens, CEPI must also ensure the work it supports does not create new risks.
What lessons from previous outbreaks convinced CEPI that biosecurity needs to be built in from the beginning?
During COVID-19, we saw that labs with stronger biorisk management were able to pivot quickly because they already had the necessary practices in place. That saved precious time and helped generate evidence needed to respond to the outbreak. The lesson for CEPI is clear: these capabilities cannot be built in the middle of a crisis--they must be established in advance.
Thatâs why CEPI takes a proactive approach to managing research risks and building readiness before a crisis. We call this biosecurity resilience. It means ensuring that labs, systems and partnerships remain capable of supporting critical research during a future outbreak. That includes reducing single points of failure and building the capacity to continue essential research swiftly even when systems are stressed, without compromising safety and security.
What role does biosecurity play in turning the 100 Days Mission into reality?",", we have to move fast, and a critical ingredient for moving fast is operating from the same rulebook, no matter where the outbreak emerges or whether it arises naturally, accidentally or deliberately. Biosecurity considerations run throughout the 100 Days Mission pathway, from laboratories developing and testing preclinical candidates, to teams evaluating early-stage clinical candidates, to the collection, testing, transfer and storage of specimens during clinical trials. A big part of our work is defining those rules before an emergency so research can move quickly and safely.
As a funder of research involving high-consequence pathogens, CEPI must also ensure the work it supports does not create new risks.
What lessons from previous outbreaks convinced CEPI that biosecurity needs to be built in from the beginning?
During COVID-19, we saw that labs with stronger biorisk management were able to pivot quickly because they already had the necessary practices in place. That saved precious time and helped generate evidence needed to respond to the outbreak. The lesson for CEPI is clear: these capabilities cannot be built in the middle of a crisis--they must be established in advance.
Thatâs why CEPI takes a proactive approach to managing research risks and building readiness before a crisis. We call this biosecurity resilience. It means ensuring that labs, systems and partnerships remain capable of supporting critical research during a future outbreak. That includes reducing single points of failure and building the capacity to continue essential research swiftly even when systems are stressed, without compromising safety and security.
What role does biosecurity play in turning the 100 Days Mission into reality?",,storage,,webpage,https://cepi.net/cepi-30-cross-cutting-themes-explained-biosecurity
