IHC Epitope/ Antigen Retrieval: HIER vs. PIE,proteinase K,P06873,Bio-Techne,Custom Formulation Quotes,,"The source reports a buffer/formulation, but its ingredients are not recognized by this simple interpretation rule set. Use the exact composition, pH, concentrations, and manufacturer's technical notes to interpret it. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.",Custom Formulation Quotes,,Not specified,No explicit refrigerated/frozen requirement or interpretable storage temperature was found in the extracted storage evidence.,,,,,,,,storage_information_not_found,"[experimental] The identification of optimal conditions for antigen retrieval requires initial testing. Once optimized, the effects of retrieval can have a significant impact on signal strength and target protein identification. Due to inconsistency in the strength of heating apparatuses employed in HIER protocols, the ideal time, temperature, and pH for each antigen should be optimized to reduce variability. For example, 5-10 minutes at 92-95 °C in a water bath may equate to 1-5 minutes at 120 °C in a pressure cooker. When antigen retrieval methods are used prior to primary antibody incubation, staining artifacts should be considered. To ensure specific staining and eliminate the possibility of artifacts, appropriate controls should be run. | [experimental] IHC images show the detection of p27 in paraffin-embedded human prostate cancer sections following incubation of tissue for 10 minutes at 95 °C in the specified antigen retrieval solution. Compared to no HIER treatment, p27 detection was enhanced following incubation in neutral (pH 7.0) and basic (pH 9.5,) but not acidic (pH 5.0) antigen retrieval solution. P27 was detected using anti-human/mouse/rat p27 monoclonal antibody (",webpage,https://www.bio-techne.com/applications/imaging/immunohistochemistry/epitope-retrieval
